Interesting challenge! One classic method is to use a tautology in the WHERE clause. For instance, instead of:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' AND password = 'letmein';

you can tweak it to:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' OR 1=1;

This way, the condition always evaluates to true, potentially granting access regardless of the password.

You can use a tautology to bypass the restriction. For instance, if the query is:
SELECT * FROM employees WHERE username = 'john';
You can change it to:
SELECT * FROM employees WHERE username = 'john' OR 1=1;
The OR 1=1 part always evaluates to true, so it returns all records.

Sure thing! If you want your WHERE clause to always evaluate as true, you can use something like 'OR 1=1'. For example:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '' OR 1=1;

This way, the query pulls all records, sidestepping any restriction.

No problem! You can inject a comment sequence to ignore the rest of the query. Try this:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' --' AND password = 'anything';

The double-dash (--) tells SQL to disregard anything after it, so it skips the password check.
